# Scene 5: split view. Reading's NASA row (y = 224, above Apollo 11's at y = 269) is
# dragged onto the Apollo 11 page on screen: once the pointer is over the page its left
# half lights in the Space's colour, with "Create split view", and the drop makes the
# split, NASA on the left, Apollo 11 on the right (splits J10, J11). The address row then
# has a part in each pane's colour, NASA's dark over the left and white over Wikipedia,
# blended over the gap (toolbar T41). The dragged row is the one NOT on screen: a page
# dropped on itself makes no split (J13), which is what the take of 2026-10-23 showed
# when the rows had moved up and the press caught the row on screen. The drag leaves the
# sidebar level with the row, so it does not pass Apollo 11's row and the list does not
# reorder on the way out. The dragmove takes 1.5 s and holds 0.5 s; the half stays lit
# about 1.5 s in all. The pointer then goes back to the sidebar's ground.
shot @MARKS@/split.png
glide 150 224 0.5
dragstart 150 224
dragmove 600 238
wait 0.5
dragend
wait 1.0
glide 150 720 0.5
